window.GENOMICS = {"order": ["stay", "blocks", "plan", "day-01", "day-02", "day-03", "day-04", "day-05", "day-06", "day-07", "day-08", "day-09", "day-10", "day-11", "day-12", "day-13", "day-14"], "pages": {"stay": {"title": "Stay on track", "kind": "guide", "html": "<p>Goal: be genuinely able to build a pipeline like this one, and talk about it in the NHS genomics interview. This file is for the days you do not feel like it, and for the walls you hit. Read it once now. Come back to it on a bad day.</p>\n<h2>1. Why the old plan broke (so we do not repeat it)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It was built like a calendar. One day missed felt like \"behind\", and \"behind\" feels bad, so more days were missed.</li>\n<li>Each day was a full hour of new, hard material with nothing easy in it.</li>\n<li>When you hit something hard there was no pre-decided next move, so you stopped.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fixes are below. None needs willpower. They are rules decided <strong>now</strong> so you do not have to decide at 2:20 pm when you are stuck.</p>\n<h2>2. The shape of the hour</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>Track</th>\n<th>What</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to 25</td>\n<td>Python block</td>\n<td>Next sitting in <code>PYTHON-BLOCKS.md</code> (a lesson on the study site)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25 to 60</td>\n<td>Project</td>\n<td>Next day page in <code>daily/</code> (each has a PAUSE POINT, so one page is about two sittings)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>You always do <strong>the next unticked item</strong> in <code>notes/tracker.md</code>. There is no \"behind\", only \"next\".</p>\n<h2>3. The minimum day (never zero)</h2>\n<p>On a bad day, a tired day, a day when you only have 20 minutes: do <strong>only</strong> this.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open a terminal in the project folder. Run <code>python check.py</code>. (2 min)</li>\n<li>Do the next Python-block sitting. (about 20 min)</li>\n<li>Write the progress line. (1 min)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That counts as a day. Tick it. <strong>The rule is: never miss two days in a row.</strong> One missed day is normal. A second one in a row is how a habit dies. If you miss one, the next day is a minimum day, not a catch-up day.</p>\n<h2>4. The brick-wall protocol</h2>\n<p>A wall = you have been stuck on the same thing for more than a couple of minutes. The motivation drop comes from not knowing what to do next. So decide it in advance:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time stuck</th>\n<th>Do this</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to 5 min</td>\n<td>Read the error message <strong>slowly, out loud</strong>. The last line says what went wrong. The line number above says where. Shrink the problem: delete code until it works, then add a line at a time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 to 10 min</td>\n<td>Print the thing you are unsure about (<code>print(x)</code>, <code>print(type(x))</code>, <code>print(df.head())</code>). Look at what it really is, not what you think it is.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 to 15 min</td>\n<td>Open the <strong>first hint</strong> in the guide. Only the first.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At 15 min</td>\n<td><strong>Stop. Write a wall note</strong> (below). Then switch to something else for the rest of the sitting, from the <em>other</em> track, or the next easy item. Do not sit and stare.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Wall note</strong> (put it at the bottom of <code>notes/progress.md</code>):</p>\n<pre><code>WALL, &lt;date&gt;: I was trying to ______. I expected ______. I got ______ (paste the last error line).\nI already tried ______ and ______.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then send it to me, in this chat. Writing the note is half the fix, because it makes you say exactly what is wrong. I will reply with the cause and the next step, not just the answer. Your job after is to <strong>retype</strong> the fix, not paste it.</p>\n<p>Two things to remember:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hitting a wall is not evidence you are bad at this. It is what the work is. Data engineers hit one every day.</li>\n<li>A wall that you solve is a <strong>win</strong>. Write it in <code>notes/wins.md</code> (next section). Those are your interview stories (\"tell me about a problem you debugged\").</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. Wins log</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/wins.md</code>. After every sitting write one line: <em>something I can do now that I could not do last week.</em> Examples: \"I wrote a ROW_NUMBER query without looking.\" \"I understood why my mask failed.\" On a bad day, <strong>read the whole file before you start</strong>. It is the evidence against the voice that says you are not getting anywhere.</p>\n<h2>6. Make starting easy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed cue.</strong> The 2:02 pm notification is the cue. When it arrives, you open the folder and the terminal <strong>before</strong> you decide whether you feel like it. Most of the resistance is in the first two minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave a trap for yourself.</strong> The last thing you do each day is write the first action for tomorrow in <code>notes/progress.md</code> and leave one test failing. You start by fixing a known thing, not by deciding what to do.</li>\n<li><strong>Short beats long.</strong> Twenty minutes every day beats two hours on Sunday. Memory needs the gaps and the repeats.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove friction.</strong> Keep the terminal and the editor open on the project folder. Pin the study site in your browser.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>7. Weekly review (10 minutes, Sundays)</h2>\n<p>On Sunday evening you get a reminder. Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and answer in three lines (to yourself, or to me in the chat):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How many days did I do something, out of 7? (Aim for 5. Do not aim for 7.)</li>\n<li>What was my biggest wall, and did I solve it?</li>\n<li>What is the one thing I will do first on Monday?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Send me those three lines if you want. I will adjust the plan if the pattern shows a problem (too long, too hard, wrong time).</p>\n<h2>8. Milestones (dates move, the order does not)</h2>\n<p>Dates assume you do about 80% of days. Missing a milestone date is information, not failure: tell me and we re-plan.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Milestone</th>\n<th>Rough date</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>M1</td>\n<td>SQL half passes checks 1 to 4, and is uploaded to GitHub</td>\n<td>Tue 15 Dec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M2</td>\n<td>Python blocks done up to pandas (lesson 3.5)</td>\n<td>Fri 18 Dec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M3</td>\n<td>Python half passes checks 5 to 9, pipeline runs</td>\n<td>Sun 10 Jan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M4</td>\n<td>Check 10, README written, repo public, mock interview done</td>\n<td>Sun 17 Jan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M5</td>\n<td>A second project of your own (Phase D)</td>\n<td>Sun 31 Jan</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>When a milestone is reached, tick it in <code>notes/tracker.md</code>, upload to GitHub, and <strong>tell me</strong>. Seeing a public repo grow is the best motivator there is.</p>\n<h2>9. Why you are doing this (write it)</h2>\n<p>At the top of <code>notes/tracker.md</code> write three sentences: <strong>Why I want the NHS genomics data engineer job.</strong> Read it when the wall is high. When the real advert is live, paste it to me and I will map each requirement to the lessons you have already done, so you can see how much is already covered.</p>\n<h2>10. What I can and cannot do</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>I can write the plan, the pages and the reminders, and fix anything that is unclear or wrong.</li>\n<li>The reminders <strong>prompt</strong> you. They cannot make you start, and they cannot see your files, so they do not know what you did.</li>\n<li>I cannot see your progress unless you tell me. A one-line message (\"did Day 3, stuck on X\", or \"missed three days\") is enough, and I will treat it as data, not as a failure.</li>\n</ul>"}, "blocks": {"title": "Python blocks: how to do a lesson", "kind": "guide", "html": "<p>Each hour starts with 25 minutes of Python, taken from your study site (the lessons you already have). The project (35 minutes) comes after. The blocks are what make the project Python steps (Step 5 onwards) doable. Skip nothing in this order.</p>\n<h2>How one lesson is done: two sittings</h2>\n<p>A lesson on the site has these sections: <strong>1. Picture, 2. Predict first, 3. Explanation, 4. Worked example, 5. Question and answer, Mixed practice (A, B, C), 6. Build it from a blank page, 7. Teach it back, 8. Self-check.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sitting A: learn it (25 min)</strong>\n| Min | Do |\n|---|---|\n| 0 to 3 | <strong>Recall.</strong> On paper, from memory, write one thing from the <em>previous</em> lesson. Then check the lesson page. |\n| 3 to 6 | Section 2, <strong>Predict first</strong>. Write your answer on paper before you reveal it. |\n| 6 to 14 | Section 3, <strong>Explanation</strong>. Read it. Do not skim. |\n| 14 to 22 | Section 4, <strong>Worked example</strong>. <strong>Type it</strong> into a file, run it, check you get the same output. |\n| 22 to 25 | Section 5, <strong>Question and answer</strong>. Answer on paper first. Tick \"Sitting A done\". |</p>\n<p><strong>Sitting B: prove it (25 min), the next day</strong>\n| Min | Do |\n|---|---|\n| 0 to 3 | <strong>Recall.</strong> Close the lesson. Write from memory the main idea and one line of code from Sitting A. |\n| 3 to 15 | <strong>Mixed practice</strong> A, B, C. Write your answer before you open anything. |\n| 15 to 23 | Section 6, <strong>Build it from a blank page</strong>. Empty file. No looking. |\n| 23 to 25 | Section 7, say the <strong>Teach it back</strong> answer out loud. Add a line to <code>notes/wins.md</code>. |</p>\n<p>If the build task in Sitting B is not finished, that is your <strong>first thing next day</strong>. Do not move on with a gap. Redoing a blank-page task two or three days later is what makes it stick. Python from a blank page is your weakest skill, so these sittings matter more than any other part.</p>\n<h2>The order (the project's critical path)</h2>\n<p>Lessons that the genomics project needs, in order. The other course lessons (classes, decorators, generators, typing, dataclasses) are valuable, but you do not need them for this project, so they come <strong>after</strong> M3.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Lesson on the site</th>\n<th>Why it is here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1.4 Conditions and loops</td>\n<td>Every function in Step 5 uses them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1.5 Functions</td>\n<td>The whole project is functions with contracts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1.6 Count items with a dictionary</td>\n<td>Same idea as <code>value_counts</code> and groupby</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>1.7 List, dict and set comprehensions</td>\n<td>Reading other people's code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>1.8 Mutable default arguments</td>\n<td>A classic interview trap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>1.9 Files: text, CSV and JSON</td>\n<td><code>read_csv</code> makes sense after this</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1.10 try/except and the order of except blocks</td>\n<td>The pipeline must fail cleanly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>1.11 Scripts, imports and virtual environments</td>\n<td><code>run_pipeline.py</code> and <code>pip install</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>2.9 Dates, times and time zones</td>\n<td>Week starts, \"old enough to judge\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>2.7 Logging</td>\n<td>Step 9 pipeline logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>3.1 DataFrames and Series</td>\n<td>Step 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>3.2 Select and filter</td>\n<td>Step 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>3.3 Group and aggregate (<strong>being written</strong>)</td>\n<td>Steps 6 and 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>3.4 Merge and join (<strong>being written</strong>)</td>\n<td>Step 6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>3.5 Rolling, shift, rank, cumsum (<strong>being written</strong>)</td>\n<td>Steps 6 and 8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Optional but recommended once M3 is done: 2.8 Testing with pytest (so you can write your own tests in Phase D), then 2.1 to 2.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Pending lessons:</strong> 3.3, 3.4 and 3.5 are not on the site yet. I am writing them next. You will not reach them for about three weeks. If you get there and they are not up, tell me.</p>\n<p>If you did lessons 1.1 to 1.3 earlier, you are starting at row 1. If you are unsure whether you did a lesson properly, do its <strong>Self-check</strong> first (section 8). If you get any wrong, redo the lesson's Sitting B.</p>\n<h2>Spaced repeat (the part that makes it stick)</h2>\n<p>Every Friday spend the 25 minutes doing a <strong>blank-page task</strong> from a lesson you did in the previous two weeks (pick the build task of any lesson, or the mixed-practice task you found hardest). If it takes more than 15 minutes, redo that lesson's Sitting B next week.</p>\n<h2>Tracking</h2>\n<p>Tick each sitting in <code>notes/tracker.md</code>.</p>"}, "plan": {"title": "Project plan overview", "kind": "guide", "html": "<p><strong>Read <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> first.</strong> It is the system that keeps you going. This file is the overview of the project track.</p>\n<h2>The shape of every hour (2 to 3 pm)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>Track</th>\n<th>What</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to 25</td>\n<td><strong>Python block</strong></td>\n<td>The next lesson sitting on the study site. See <code>PYTHON-BLOCKS.md</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25 to 60</td>\n<td><strong>Project</strong></td>\n<td>The next day page in <code>daily/</code>. Each page has a PAUSE POINT about 30 minutes in, so one page takes about two sittings.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The old rule 'one session = one day' is gone. <strong>Sessions are an ordered list. You always do the next unticked item in <code>notes/tracker.md</code>.</strong> Missing a day moves nothing else. There is no 'behind', only 'next'.</p>\n<p>The 42 sessions below are the project track, in order. Rough finish at ~80% of days done: see the milestone dates in <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Start here for the project: open the day page in the <code>daily/</code> folder (Day 1 is <code>daily/day-01.md</code>).</strong> The list below is the overview.</p>\n<h2>Rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stop at 3 pm, even mid-step.</strong> Leave a failing test on purpose and write down the next action. Tomorrow starts in 30 seconds, not 15 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Stuck for 15 minutes?</strong> Open the hints one at a time, in order. Not before.</li>\n<li><strong>A check fails and you think the check is wrong?</strong> Tell your tutor. Do not edit the checks.</li>\n<li><strong>If you run over on a step:</strong> the 'Step' sessions are guesses. Take another session. Do not rush a step to keep the date.</li>\n<li><strong>If you miss a few days:</strong> read your last progress line, run <code>python check.py</code>, and carry on. Do not re-do old steps. If you miss more than a week, spend the first 15 minutes re-reading your own code.</li>\n<li><strong>Python from a blank page is your weakest skill.</strong> That is why the Python sessions are 2 to 3 hours of work split in pieces, and why Phase D exists. Do not skip the blank-page sessions.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep one file open:</strong> <code>gotchas-cheatsheet.md</code>. Add every mistake you make to it, in your own words.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What the checker can and cannot do</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It tells you whether your answer is right, one failure at a time. It cannot tell you whether your code is clear.</li>\n<li>SQL checks run your T-SQL in DuckDB, not SQL Server. They test logic. Phase E (optional) is where SQL Server comes in, and I have not run that step on a real server.</li>\n<li>Project sessions 15 to 19 repeat the pandas lessons that are also in your Python block track (<code>PYTHON-BLOCKS.md</code>). If you did them there, tick them here and skip. Both rely on Module 3 lessons. 3.1 and 3.2 are on your site now. <strong>3.3, 3.4 and 3.5 are not written yet.</strong> I will write them while you do Phase A. If you reach session 17 and they are not there, tell me.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The sessions</h2>\n<h3>Phase 0: Set up and understand the data</h3>\n<p><strong>Session 1: Set up and read the story</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-01\">Day 1</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unzip the project. In a terminal inside the folder: pip install -r requirements.txt, then python check.py (Windows: py). Every step should say 'not yet'. That is correct.</li>\n<li>Read START-HERE.md, then guide/00_the_story_and_the_data.md.</li>\n<li>On paper, draw the four stages: referred, sample received, sequenced, reported. Under it write: capacity, backlog, turnaround in one line each.</li>\n<li>Open data/ and look at the raw CSV in a text editor. Do not open it in Excel yet. Excel changes dates and drops leading zeros.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You can say the business problem in 3 sentences without looking. python check.py runs and shows all steps 'not yet'.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 2: Step 1: look at the data (questions 1 to 5)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-02\">Day 2</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read guide/01 'Think first'. Write your guesses on paper BEFORE you look at any numbers.</li>\n<li>Read 'The tools'. Use run_sql.py or pandas to explore.</li>\n<li>Answer questions 1 to 5 in notes/findings.py (rows, duplicates, distinct ids, first and last date, messy test types).</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Questions 1 to 5 filled in. Compare your paper guesses with the real numbers. Note one thing that surprised you.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 3: Step 1: finish and check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-03\">Day 3</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Answer questions 6 to 10 in notes/findings.py.</li>\n<li>python check.py 1. Fix only the FIRST message, run again.</li>\n<li>Say the 'Say it aloud' answer out loud, once, to the wall.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 1 passes. You can explain why 'empty' is not always a fault.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase A: SQL (Steps 2 to 4). Needs no pandas</h3>\n<p><strong>Session 4: Step 2: read, think, run the library example</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-04\">Day 4</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read guide/02 'Think first' and answer on paper. Read 'The T-SQL trap you must know'.</li>\n<li>Run guide/examples/run_library_sql.py. Read library_sql.sql slowly. Do not copy it. It is the same idea on a different subject.</li>\n<li>Open sql/02_clean_load.sql and read the brief twice. Write the six build parts (the guide lists them) on a sticky note.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You can explain: why the raw table is all text, why TRY_CONVERT, why '' must go through NULLIF first. No code written yet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 5: Step 2: select, convert, spell (parts 1 to 3)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-05\">Day 5</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 1: select the columns as trimmed text. Run with python run_sql.py.</li>\n<li>Part 2: add the type conversions with TRY_CONVERT, with NULLIF inside every one.</li>\n<li>Part 3: standard spellings for test type and priority (CASE).</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> run_sql.py prints sensible rows. Dates are dates, bad dates are NULL, not errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 6: Step 2: ROW_NUMBER and reject reasons (parts 4 and 5)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-06\">Day 6</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 4: ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY id ORDER BY ...) and keep one row per id.</li>\n<li>Part 5: RejectReason with CASE. First reason wins, so the order of WHEN lines matters.</li>\n<li>Hints are one at a time and only after 15 stuck minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Duplicates are dropped, and bad rows have a reason. Count the reasons and see if it matches your Step 1 notes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 7: Step 2: finish, check, compare</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-07\">Day 7</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 6: the view, the two INSERTs and the count SELECT.</li>\n<li>python check.py 2. First message only.</li>\n<li>Open the finished project's version and compare. Write one entry in notes/decisions.md. Say it aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 2 passes. One decisions.md entry written.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 8: Step 3: weekly table (pattern, parts 1 and 2)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-08\">Day 8</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read guide/03 'Think first' (what is a backlog as a sum?). Run the library pattern.</li>\n<li>Read why the Monday-of-week columns use DATEDIFF(DAY, 0, ...) and not DATEPART(WEEKDAY, ...).</li>\n<li>Part 1: referrals per week as one CTE. Part 2: the other three measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Four weekly counts, each run on its own and looking right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 9: Step 3: join capacity and utilisation (parts 3 and 4)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-09\">Day 9</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 3: LEFT JOIN to dbo.CapacityWeekly. Keep only the first 78 weeks.</li>\n<li>Part 4: utilisation = work done / capacity. Watch for integer division (the check looks for it).</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> 78 rows. A week with no referrals still shows, with 0, not missing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 10: Step 3: backlog and the 4-week average (part 5), check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-10\">Day 10</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 5: backlog = cumulative samples received minus cumulative sequenced, using a window with a frame. Then the 4-week rolling average.</li>\n<li>python check.py 3. Compare with the finished version. decisions.md entry. Say it aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 3 passes. You can explain ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW in your own words.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 11: Step 4: eligible referrals</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-11\">Day 11</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read guide/04 'Think first' (fairness: is a 10-day-old routine referral without a result late?). Write the rule in your own words.</li>\n<li>Build the view of ELIGIBLE referrals only (old enough to judge). Targets: urgent 14 days, routine 42 days. They are assumptions, not NHS standards.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> The view holds only referrals old enough to judge. You can say why the latest months would look terrible otherwise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 12: Step 4: percentage and median/p90</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-12\">Day 12</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Join to the small rules table. SUM(CASE ...) for the share within target. Watch integer division.</li>\n<li>PERCENTILE_CONT for the median and 90th percentile.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Percentage and the two percentiles come out by month and priority.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 13: Step 4: check and Stage A review</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-13\">Day 13</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>python check.py 4. Compare. decisions.md entry. Say it aloud.</li>\n<li>Run python check.py. Steps 1 to 4 should be green. Read guide/04 'Stage A is done'.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Steps 1 to 4 all pass. Stage A (SQL) is finished.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 14: Blank-page session: rebuild a piece from memory</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Full step-by-step page: <a href=\"#/genomics/day-14\">Day 14</a></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Close all guides. Open an empty .sql file. Write from memory the ROW_NUMBER keep-one-per-id CTE for dbo.Referrals_Raw (just that part).</li>\n<li>Stuck? Look at the library example, not your own answer. Then write it again without looking.</li>\n<li>Write on paper the five mistakes you made most in Steps 2 to 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You wrote it without looking. You have a written list of your five most common mistakes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase B: pandas, then Python (Steps 5 to 9)</h3>\n<p><strong>Session 15: Course lesson 3.1: DataFrames and Series</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>On the study site: lesson 3.1. Do predict, read, run the example, do the three mixed tasks A to C, then the blank-page build task.</li>\n<li>Type every example. Do not paste.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You did the 3.1 build task from a blank page. Tick 3.1 in the tracker.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 16: Course lesson 3.2: select and filter</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesson 3.2: masks, &amp;, |, ~, isin, .str, .loc, .copy().</li>\n<li>Do the mixed tasks and the build task from a blank page.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> 3.2 build task done from a blank page. Say what a mask is in 3 sentences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 17: Course lesson 3.3: groupby and aggregates (being written)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesson 3.3 (groupby, named aggregation, value_counts, reindex). This lesson is not on the site yet. Ask your tutor to write it before this session. Do not skip it.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> 3.3 done. Tick it in the tracker.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 18: Course lesson 3.4: merge (being written)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesson 3.4 (merge and join). Not on the site yet. Ask your tutor for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> 3.4 done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 19: Course lesson 3.5: rolling, shift, rank, cumsum (being written)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesson 3.5. Not on the site yet. Step 6 needs cumsum and rolling. Ask your tutor for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> 3.5 done. You are ready for Step 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 20: Step 5: read, think, pattern</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>guide/05 'Think first' on paper. Run the library_python.py pattern.</li>\n<li>Read dcap/clean.py: the four stub functions and their docstring contracts. Say in your own words what each must return.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You can explain why the cleaning is four small functions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 21: Step 5: load_referrals and normalise_text</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write load_referrals (dtype and parse_dates) and normalise_text (strip, case, spellings).</li>\n<li>python check.py 5 and read only the first failure. normalise_text must not change its input (use .copy()).</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> The first six or seven checks in step 5 pass.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 22: Step 5: find_reject_reasons</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First reason wins. Think: how do you make 'first one that is true' with a column in pandas? (Hints in the guide, one at a time.)</li>\n<li>python check.py 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> The reject-reason checks pass, including same-day dates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 23: Step 5: clean_referrals, check, compare</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write clean_referrals: duplicates, reject reasons, keep rejects, reset the index.</li>\n<li>python check.py 5 all green. Compare. decisions.md. Say it aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 5 passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 24: Step 6: week_start and count_by_week</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>guide/06 'Think first'. Pattern. Write week_start (the Monday of a date's week) and count_by_week.</li>\n<li>python check.py 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> week_start and count_by_week tests pass.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 25: Step 6: weekly_table</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write weekly_table: counts, capacity join, utilisation, cumsum backlog, rolling 4-week average. Same table as your SQL Step 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> weekly_table values test passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 26: Step 6: finish, compare, buffer</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Finish check 6. Compare. decisions.md entry. Say it aloud. Use spare time to re-read your own code as a stranger would.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 6 passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 27: Step 7: turnaround in pandas</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>guide/07 'Think first'. Write turnaround_by_cohort: masks, groupby().agg() with named results, quantile, dt.to_period.</li>\n<li>python check.py 7. Compare. decisions.md. Say it aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 7 passes. Same fairness rule as SQL Step 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 28: Step 8: forecast, the three methods</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>guide/08 'Think first'. Write your guess for the winning method on paper.</li>\n<li>Write naive_last, moving_average_4, linear_trend_26.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> The three-methods test passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 29: Step 8: backtest and forecast_demand</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write backtest (HOLDOUT_WEEKS = 12) and forecast_demand. Which method won? Was your guess right?</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> The backtest tests pass.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 30: Step 8: capacity gap and weeks to clear</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write capacity_gap and weeks_to_clear. python check.py 8. Compare. decisions.md. Say it aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 8 passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 31: Step 9: the pipeline (main)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>guide/09 'Think first'. Write main() in run_pipeline.py as small calls to the functions you wrote. Add logging.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> python run_pipeline.py runs from start to finish.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 32: Step 9: check and read your own output</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>python check.py 9. Look at the files in output/: charts, reject file, row counts. Is anything odd?</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 9 passes. You can explain what each output file is for.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase C: prove it and present it (Steps 10 and 11)</h3>\n<p><strong>Session 33: Step 10: prove SQL and Python agree</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>python run_pipeline.py, then python check.py 10. If they disagree, one is wrong. Find which. The guide has a 'If a check fails' list.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> check 10 passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 34: Step 11: README part 1</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Run the pipeline. Fill every [YOUR WORDS] in README.md using YOUR numbers. Keep the synthetic-data warning and the 'targets are assumptions' line.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> README has no [YOUR WORDS] left.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 35: Step 11: 60-second story and interview questions</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write your 60-second story. Answer the Part 3 questions out loud, then in writing for the two you found hardest.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You can tell the project in 60 seconds without notes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 36: Step 11: be honest, mock interview, upload</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read Part 4 (be honest about how you built it). Do Part 5 (the mock interview).</li>\n<li>Upload to GitHub using the web UI steps at the end of guide/11.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> The repo is public and the README renders. python check.py all is green.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phase D: build a similar project yourself (the real test)</h3>\n<p><strong>Session 37: Pick a new theme and write the brief</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick a different service with demand, capacity and waiting: imaging scans, outpatient clinic, a help desk, a call centre.</li>\n<li>Write a one-page brief: the question, the stages, the columns you would need, the three measures, what 'old enough to judge' means for you. No code.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> A one-page brief you could hand to someone else.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 38: Make the data and look at it</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adapt make_data.py in the finished project to make a small messy file for your theme (or ask your tutor for one). Do Step 1 on it: ten questions of your own.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You wrote ten findings with numbers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 39: Clean it, in SQL or Python (your choice)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Build the clean step from a blank page. Write your own tiny tests with 4-row tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Your clean step passes your own tests. Rejects are kept with a reason.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 40: Weekly table</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Build the weekly demand, capacity, backlog table from a blank page.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Your weekly table matches a hand-calculated week.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 41: Turnaround and forecast</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Build the turnaround measure with the fairness rule, and a backtested forecast.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You can say which method won and by how much.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Session 42: Pipeline, README and a 10-minute talk</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>One command runs it. Write a short README. Explain it out loud for 10 minutes, as in an interview.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> You built something similar from a blank page, with your own tests. That is the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Optional, after course Module 4: run it on SQL Server</h3>\n<p><strong>Session 43: Step 12: SQL Server (optional)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Full day page: being written. It will appear in <code>daily/</code> before you reach it.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Follow guide/12_stage_c_sql_server.md. You need a SQL Server you may break (Developer edition or Docker), never a work or live server. This step has no automatic check and I have not run it on a real server.</li>\n<li><strong>Done when:</strong> Your T-SQL ran on a real server and the weekly table matches your Python output.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>After the plan</h2>\n<p>You will be able to: clean messy data with kept rejects; build weekly demand, capacity and backlog in SQL and in pandas; judge turnaround fairly; backtest a forecast; wire a logged pipeline; and prove two versions agree. That is the shape of most junior data engineering work.</p>"}, "day-01": {"title": "Day 1: Set up and understand the story", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Get the project running on your laptop and understand the business problem. You write no code today.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>README</code>-type reading only. You create nothing except one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start</h2>\n<p>Today there is no daily routine yet. First you set up the project (next section).</p>\n<h2>5 to 20 min: Unzip and install (one time only)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Find <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided.zip</code>. Right-click it, choose <strong>Extract All</strong>, and pick a folder you will find again, for example <code>Documents\\projects</code>. You now have a folder called <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code>.</li>\n<li>Open a terminal <strong>inside that folder</strong>: open the folder in File Explorer, click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type this and press Enter:\n   <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>\n   (If <code>pip</code> is not recognized: <code>py -m pip install -r requirements.txt</code>.)\n   Lots of text scrolls past. It should end with <strong>Successfully installed ...</strong> or <strong>Requirement already satisfied</strong>. That is fine.</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. You should see a list called <strong>YOUR PROGRESS</strong>: Step 1 to Step 10, every one saying <strong>not yet</strong>. That is correct. Nothing is built yet.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>If something fails:</strong> copy the last 10 lines of the red/error text into a message to me. Do not spend more than 10 minutes on it. A work laptop can block <code>pip</code>. If it does, tell me.</p>\n<h2>20 to 35 min: Read</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <code>START-HERE.md</code> and read it all. Pay attention to the table of steps and the section <strong>How each step works</strong>.</li>\n<li>Open <code>guide/00_the_story_and_the_data.md</code> and read it all. It explains the lab, the data and the checker.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 1: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>35 to 50 min: Draw it on paper</h2>\n<p>On a sheet of paper, with no looking:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw the four stages in a row with arrows: <strong>referred, sample received, sequenced, reported</strong>.</li>\n<li>Under the drawing, write one sentence each, in YOUR words, for: <strong>capacity</strong>, <strong>backlog</strong>, <strong>turnaround</strong>.</li>\n<li>Now check your sentences against <code>guide/00</code> (the sections \"The business problem\" and \"The data\"). Add anything you missed in a different colour. Do not rewrite it neatly. The mistakes are the useful part.</li>\n<li>Write the five questions the service manager asks (they are numbered 1 to 5 in guide/00) on the paper. You will answer all five by Day 32.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>50 to 57 min: Look at the raw files (do NOT use Excel)</h2>\n<p>Excel changes dates and drops leading zeros. Use a text editor.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In your text editor choose File &gt; Open and open <code>data/referrals_raw.csv</code>. Look at the first 20 lines.</li>\n<li>Answer on paper: <strong>How many columns? Which ones hold dates? Which cells are empty? Does anything look odd to you?</strong> Write at least two oddities or \"nothing yet\".</li>\n<li>Open <code>data/capacity_weekly.csv</code>. Same questions, shorter.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do not count anything yet. Day 2 starts with counting.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 1, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 2: open `daily/day-02.md`. Write my guesses, then count rows.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-02\">Day 2 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-02": {"title": "Day 2: Step 1: look at the data (questions 1 to 5)", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Answer the first five of the ten questions about the raw file, with SQL.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>notes/findings.py</code> (you edit it), <code>notes/progress.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 15 min: Guess first (this is the habit)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code>. At the bottom, under a new heading <strong>My guesses</strong>, write your answers to these <strong>before</strong> you look at any numbers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How many rows do you think are in <code>referrals_raw.csv</code>? (A number, a rough one is fine.)</li>\n<li>Is every <code>referral_id</code> unique? (yes or no)</li>\n<li>What do you think is wrong with the file? (You were told it is dirty.)</li>\n<li>Which columns can be empty, and is empty always a fault?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Save. Now you can look. (Why? If you only look, you never notice when you are surprised.)</p>\n<h2>15 to 20 min: Meet the file you will edit</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/findings.py</code>. You will see ten lines like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">rows_in_file = None                       # data rows in referrals_raw.csv (not counting the header line)\nexact_duplicate_rows = None\ndistinct_referral_ids = None\nfirst_referred_date = None\nlast_referred_date = None\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>Your job over Days 2 and 3 is to replace each <code>None</code> with the answer. A number goes in as a number (<code>rows_in_file = 123</code>). A date goes in as text in quotes (<code>first_referred_date = \"2025-01-06\"</code> is the <em>shape</em>, not the answer).</p>\n<p>Today: the first <strong>five</strong>: <code>rows_in_file</code>, <code>exact_duplicate_rows</code>, <code>distinct_referral_ids</code>, <code>first_referred_date</code>, <code>last_referred_date</code>.</p>\n<h2>20 to 50 min: Count with SQL</h2>\n<p>You query a table called <code>dbo.Referrals_Raw</code>. Every column in it is text. Its columns are <code>ReferralID, ReferredDate, TestType, Priority, SampleReceivedDate, SequencedDate, ReportedDate</code>. You run a query like this, from the terminal, in the project folder:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Question 1: how many data rows are in the file (not counting the header line)?</strong>\nThe query above counts the rows in the table. Run it. Write the number in <code>notes/findings.py</code> as <code>rows_in_file</code>. Save the file.</p>\n<p><strong>Question 2: how many rows are an exact copy of an earlier row?</strong> (\"Exact\" means every one of the 7 columns is the same.)\nRun this:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw) AS d&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>SELECT DISTINCT *</code> keeps one of each different row. So: <strong>rows in file minus this number = exact duplicates.</strong> Do the subtraction on paper. Write it as <code>exact_duplicate_rows</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Question 3: how many different <code>referral_id</code> values are there?</strong></p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT ReferralID) AS n FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Write it as <code>distinct_referral_ids</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Look at your own three numbers.</strong> Is the third number the same as the \"distinct rows\" number from Question 2? If yes, what does that tell you about the repeated rows? (Write one sentence on paper. This matters on Day 6.)</p>\n<p><strong>Questions 4 and 5: the first and last referred date.</strong> Dates here are text. Sorting text only works as dates if <strong>every date has the same format</strong>. Check that first:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT LEN(ReferredDate) AS length_of_text, COUNT(*) AS n FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw GROUP BY LEN(ReferredDate)&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you get <strong>one row</strong> (all the same length, 10 characters, like <code>2025-01-06</code>) then text sorting is safe. Then:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT MIN(ReferredDate) AS first_date, MAX(ReferredDate) AS last_date FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Write the two dates <strong>in quotes</strong> into <code>first_referred_date</code> and <code>last_referred_date</code>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 2: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>50 to 57 min: Check</h2>\n<p>In the terminal:</p>\n<pre><code>python check.py 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>The check stops at the <strong>first</strong> problem and shows only that one. Today the right result is a message saying <strong><code>rows_with_messy_test_type is not answered yet</code></strong>. That means your first five all passed (that item is Question 6, which is tomorrow). If instead it names one of your five, with \"That is not right. Look again at how you counted\", fix that one only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did you write a date without quotes, or a number with quotes?</li>\n<li>For Question 2, did you do rows minus distinct rows (not the other way round)?</li>\n<li>Run the query again and read the number carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Compare with your <strong>My guesses</strong> in <code>progress.md</code>. Write one line under them: <strong>Which guess was wrong? What surprised me?</strong></p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 2, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 3: open `daily/day-03.md`. Questions 6 to 10 in `notes/findings.py`.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-01\">&lt; Day 1</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-03\">Day 3 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-03": {"title": "Day 3: Step 1: finish and check", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Answer questions 6 to 10 and pass check 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>notes/findings.py</code>, <code>notes/decisions.md</code>, <code>notes/progress.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 10 min: Where you are</h2>\n<p><code>python check.py 1</code> should currently stop at <code>rows_with_messy_test_type is not answered yet</code> (your first five are right). Open <code>notes/findings.py</code> and find the five <code>None</code> lines left: <code>rows_with_messy_test_type</code>, <code>rows_reported_before_sequenced</code>, <code>rows_without_sample_received_date</code>, <code>weeks_in_capacity_file</code>, <code>lowest_weekly_capacity</code>.</p>\n<h2>10 to 25 min: Question 6, the messy test type</h2>\n<p><strong>Question 6: how many rows have a <code>TestType</code> that is not EXACTLY <code>Rare disease</code>, <code>Cancer panel</code> or <code>Rapid</code>?</strong></p>\n<p>First <strong>look</strong> at the values. Spaces are invisible, so put square brackets round them:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT CONCAT('[', TestType, ']') AS shown, COUNT(*) AS n FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw GROUP BY TestType ORDER BY TestType&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>You see six rows. Read each one. Which are exactly right? Which have a different case, or a space inside the brackets? Add up the <code>n</code> of <strong>only the wrong ones</strong>. That is your answer. Write it as <code>rows_with_messy_test_type</code>.</p>\n<p>(Why does this matter? If you filter on <code>TestType = 'Cancer panel'</code> later, the messy rows silently disappear.)</p>\n<h2>25 to 40 min: Questions 7 and 8 (use your own SQL)</h2>\n<p>Both questions say \"after removing exact duplicates\". So start from this, which keeps one of each exact row:</p>\n<pre><code>(SELECT DISTINCT * FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw) AS d\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Question 7: how many rows have <code>reported_date</code> earlier than <code>sequenced_date</code>?</strong>\nBoth are text. Make a file called <code>my_query.sql</code> in the project folder (File &gt; New, Save As, in the project folder) and fill in the two blanks:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw) AS d\nWHERE ______  &lt;  ______ ;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Each blank must turn the text column into a real date: <code>TRY_CONVERT(DATE, NULLIF(d.ReportedDate, ''))</code>. The <code>NULLIF(x, '')</code> turns an empty cell into NULL first. (Day 4 explains why this matters on SQL Server. Just write it this way.) Run it:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py my_query.sql\n</code></pre>\n<p>Write the number as <code>rows_reported_before_sequenced</code>. Stuck for 15 minutes? Read <strong>Hint 3</strong> in <code>guide/01_look_at_the_data.md</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Question 8: how many rows have an empty <code>sample_received_date</code>?</strong>\nChange <code>my_query.sql</code> to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw) AS d\nWHERE NULLIF(LTRIM(RTRIM(d.SampleReceivedDate)), '') IS NULL;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run it the same way. (An empty cell can arrive as NULL or as empty text depending on how it was loaded. This test catches both.) Write the number as <code>rows_without_sample_received_date</code>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 3: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>40 to 47 min: Questions 9 and 10 (capacity file)</h2>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py &quot;SELECT COUNT(*) AS n, MIN(SequencingCapacity) AS lowest FROM dbo.CapacityWeekly&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Two numbers: write <code>n</code> as <code>weeks_in_capacity_file</code> and <code>lowest</code> as <code>lowest_weekly_capacity</code>.</p>\n<h2>47 to 57 min: Check, then think</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code>python check.py 1</code>. All ten should pass. If not, it names the first one that is wrong or empty: fix only that one.</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/decisions.md</code>. Under <strong>## Step 2: cleaning</strong> replace <code>(your entries)</code> with one entry in this form (the file shows an example at the top):\n   - <strong>Choice:</strong> (what you will do about the duplicate rows)\n   - <strong>Why:</strong>\n   - <strong>Other option:</strong></li>\n<li>Say this out loud once, with your real numbers: <em>\"Before I wrote any code I profiled the raw file: row counts, duplicates, odd spellings, impossible date orders. I found X, Y and Z. That decided what my cleaning step does.\"</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 3, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 4: open `daily/day-04.md`. Read guide/02 and run the library example.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-02\">&lt; Day 2</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-04\">Day 4 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-04": {"title": "Day 4: Step 2 (SQL clean): read and learn the pattern", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Understand exactly what you will build on Days 5 to 7, using a different subject (a library).</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>guide/02_sql_clean.md</code>, <code>guide/examples/library_sql.sql</code>, <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code> (read only today).</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 20 min: Think first, on paper</h2>\n<p>Open <code>guide/02_sql_clean.md</code>. Read only down to <strong>The pattern</strong>. Answer these three on paper in your own words:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The raw table holds <strong>only text</strong>. Why is that a good idea for a load? (What happens to the whole load if one date says <code>31/02/2025</code>?)</li>\n<li>A row is bad. You can delete it, fix it, or keep it aside with a reason. Which one lets you <strong>explain the numbers later</strong>?</li>\n<li>A row breaks two rules. How many reasons should it get? Which one?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check yourself against the \"Say it aloud\" paragraph at the bottom of the guide <strong>after</strong> you have written your answers.</p>\n<h2>20 to 40 min: Run and read the library example</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In the terminal: <code>python guide/examples/run_library_sql.py</code>. It prints several small tables. Find the one that lists reject reasons.</li>\n<li>Open <code>guide/examples/library_sql.sql</code>. Read <strong>Pattern 1</strong> only (the first view, <code>vw_LoansChecked</code>). Find these <strong>five ideas</strong>, and put a finger (or a comment on paper) on each:</li>\n<li>The <code>Typed</code> CTE turns text into types in one place.</li>\n<li><code>CASE LOWER(LTRIM(RTRIM(x))) WHEN ... END</code> gives standard spellings. There is <strong>no ELSE</strong>, so an unknown value becomes NULL.</li>\n<li><code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY id ORDER BY ...)</code> numbers the copies of each id. <code>WHERE RowInId = 1</code> keeps one.</li>\n<li>A <code>CASE WHEN ... THEN 'reason'</code> gives one reason per row. The <strong>first true WHEN wins</strong>, so the order of the lines matters.</li>\n<li><code>RejectReason IS NULL</code> means \"good row\".</li>\n<li>Close the file. Do not copy anything.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 4: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>40 to 52 min: Read your brief</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code>. The whole brief is a comment (between <code>/*</code> and <code>*/</code>) at the top. Read it twice.</li>\n<li>On paper, write the <strong>8 reject reasons in order</strong> (this order is what you will type on Day 6). Under each, write which column it is about.</li>\n<li>Read the box <strong>The T-SQL trap you must know</strong> in <code>guide/02</code>. In one sentence: why must every date go through <code>NULLIF(x, '')</code> before <code>TRY_CONVERT</code>? (On SQL Server converting empty text to a date gives 1900-01-01, not NULL. The checker cannot see this mistake, so only you can prevent it.)</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>52 to 57 min: Read the official words (optional, 5 minutes)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/try-convert-transact-sql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TRY_CONVERT</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/nullif-transact-sql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NULLIF</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/row-number-transact-sql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ROW_NUMBER</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>(These are Microsoft's own pages. I confirmed the links exist but have not read every line of them.)</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 4, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 5: open `daily/day-05.md`. Create `scratch/step2.sql` and write Part 1.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-03\">&lt; Day 3</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-05\">Day 5 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-05": {"title": "Day 5: Step 2: select, convert, spell (parts 1 to 3)", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Write the first half of the cleaning query, in a scratch file, and check it with real numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> New file <code>scratch/step2.sql</code> (you create it). <code>notes/progress.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 10 min: Make your workbench</h2>\n<p>You will build the query in a scratch file first, because <code>run_sql.py</code> can run a plain <code>SELECT</code> but not the final view-plus-<code>GO</code> script. (The final script is tested with <code>python check.py 2</code> on Day 7.)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In the project folder create a new folder called <code>scratch</code>.</li>\n<li>Inside it create a file called <code>step2.sql</code> (in your editor: New file, Save As <code>scratch\\step2.sql</code>).</li>\n<li>You run it with: <code>python run_sql.py scratch/step2.sql</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>10 to 25 min: Part 1. Select the columns as trimmed text</h2>\n<p>Type (do not paste) this start into <code>scratch/step2.sql</code> and then finish the other six columns yourself in the same style:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH Typed AS (\n    SELECT\n        LTRIM(RTRIM(r.ReferralID)) AS ReferralID\n        -- add the other six columns here, each trimmed, with the same names as the raw table\n    FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw AS r\n)\nSELECT * FROM Typed;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run it. You get a table with 8,717 rows (it prints a lot; only the first and last lines matter). Then change the last line to this and run again:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM Typed;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see 8717.</strong> If not, did you leave out <code>FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw AS r</code>?</p>\n<h2>25 to 40 min: Part 2. Real dates</h2>\n<p>Change the four date columns (ReferredDate, SampleReceivedDate, SequencedDate, ReportedDate) from trimmed text into real dates:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">TRY_CONVERT(DATE, NULLIF(r.ReferredDate, '')) AS ReferredDate\n</code></pre>\n<p>Always the same shape: <code>TRY_CONVERT(DATE, NULLIF(&lt;column&gt;, ''))</code>. <strong>Every date column, no exceptions.</strong> Then check with this final line:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT\n    COUNT(*) AS rows_,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN ReferredDate       IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS no_referred,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN SampleReceivedDate IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS no_sample,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN SequencedDate      IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS no_sequenced,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN ReportedDate       IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS no_reported\nFROM Typed;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see:</strong> <code>rows_</code> 8717, <code>no_referred</code> 0, <code>no_sample</code> 78, <code>no_sequenced</code> 181, <code>no_reported</code> 413.\nIf a number is different: did one column miss <code>NULLIF</code>? Is a column name mixed up?</p>\n<p>(Those empty counts are <em>not</em> faults. A referral that has not been sequenced yet is normal. Think about why.)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 5: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>40 to 55 min: Part 3. Standard spellings</h2>\n<p>Replace the <code>TestType</code> and <code>Priority</code> columns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>TestType</code> must be exactly <code>Rare disease</code>, <code>Cancer panel</code> or <code>Rapid</code>. Use <code>CASE LOWER(LTRIM(RTRIM(r.TestType))) WHEN 'rare disease' THEN 'Rare disease' ... END</code>. <strong>No <code>ELSE</code>.</strong> (Anything unknown must become NULL, so the next part can reject it.)</li>\n<li><code>Priority</code>: trim and lower-case it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check with this final line:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT TestType, COUNT(*) AS n FROM Typed GROUP BY TestType ORDER BY TestType;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see exactly three rows</strong> and no NULL: <code>Cancer panel 3042</code>, <code>Rapid 1311</code>, <code>Rare disease 4364</code>. (Compare with Day 3: the messy spellings have been folded into the right ones. 3041 + 1 = 3042.)\nThen:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT Priority, COUNT(*) AS n FROM Typed GROUP BY Priority ORDER BY Priority;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see:</strong> <code>routine 7448</code>, <code>urgent 1269</code>.</p>\n<h2>55 to 57 min: Save</h2>\n<p>Do not delete <code>scratch/step2.sql</code>. You continue tomorrow.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 5, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 6: open `daily/day-06.md`. Add the ROW_NUMBER and the RejectReason to `scratch/step2.sql`.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-04\">&lt; Day 4</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-06\">Day 6 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-06": {"title": "Day 6: Step 2: ROW_NUMBER and reject reasons (parts 4 and 5)", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Keep one row per referral id, and give each bad row exactly one reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>scratch/step2.sql</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 10 min: Think</h2>\n<p>Reread your Day 3 sentence: <em>the repeated rows are exact copies</em>. So which copy you keep does not change the result. But SQL has no \"first row in the file\". So <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> needs an <code>ORDER BY</code> that makes the choice <strong>repeatable</strong>. If a repeat ever had different data, what would you want to happen? (Write one line. It goes into <code>notes/decisions.md</code> on Day 7.)</p>\n<h2>10 to 30 min: Part 4. One row per id</h2>\n<p>Open <code>scratch/step2.sql</code>. After the <code>Typed</code> CTE add a second CTE (put a comma after the first one's closing bracket):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">Numbered AS (\n    SELECT t.*,\n           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY t.ReferralID ORDER BY ______) AS RowInId\n    FROM Typed AS t\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Fill the <code>ORDER BY</code> blank with the date columns (any order is fine; pick the one you can defend). Then check:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT RowInId, COUNT(*) AS n FROM Numbered GROUP BY RowInId ORDER BY RowInId;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see:</strong> <code>1  8692</code> and <code>2  25</code>. That is 8,692 different ids, and 25 repeats. Does that match your Day 2 numbers? It must.</p>\n<p>Then keep only the first of each: the final query becomes <code>SELECT ... FROM Numbered WHERE RowInId = 1</code>. Check:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM Numbered WHERE RowInId = 1;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see 8692.</strong></p>\n<h2>30 to 52 min: Part 5. The RejectReason column</h2>\n<p>In the final <code>SELECT</code> (the one that reads from <code>Numbered</code>) add this column. Write the eight lines in <strong>the order from the brief</strong> (the order you wrote on paper yesterday). The first <code>WHEN</code> that is true wins:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CASE\n    WHEN n.TestType IS NULL THEN 'unknown test_type'\n    WHEN ______ THEN 'unknown priority'\n    ...six more...\nEND AS RejectReason\n</code></pre>\n<p>The exact wording of each reason is in the brief at the top of <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code> (copy the words exactly; the checker compares them letter by letter).</p>\n<p>Three things to think about while you write them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>n.SampleReceivedDate &lt; n.ReferredDate</code> is simply <strong>false</strong> when either date is NULL. Why is that handy here?</li>\n<li>\"sequenced without sample received\" is different. There the later date <strong>is present</strong> and the earlier <strong>is NULL</strong>. Write that test with <code>IS NOT NULL</code> and <code>IS NULL</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code>&lt;</code>, not <code>&lt;=</code>. A result reported on the same day as sequencing is fine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check: turn your final select into a CTE called <code>Checked</code> (a third CTE after <code>Numbered</code>, containing the <code>SELECT ... CASE ... END AS RejectReason ... WHERE RowInId = 1</code>), then end the file with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT RejectReason, COUNT(*) AS n FROM Checked GROUP BY RejectReason ORDER BY RejectReason;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>You should see:</strong> one row with RejectReason empty (NULL) and <code>n</code> = 8677, and one row <code>reported_date before sequenced_date</code> with <code>n</code> = 15. Total 8,692.\n(Only one rule fires on the real file. The other seven are tested by the checker with tiny made-up tables on Day 7. This is why test tables matter: real data does not exercise every rule.)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 6: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>52 to 57 min: Stuck?</h2>\n<p>Stuck for 15 minutes on the same thing? Open the hints in <code>guide/02_sql_clean.md</code> <strong>one at a time, in order</strong>: Hint 2 for ROW_NUMBER, Hint 3 for the reasons. Not before.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 6, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 7: open `daily/day-07.md`. Turn the scratch query into the view, add the two INSERTs and the count SELECT in `sql/02_clean_load.sql`.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-05\">&lt; Day 5</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-07\">Day 7 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-07": {"title": "Day 7: Step 2: finish, check, compare", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Move your query into the real file, add the loads, and pass <code>python check.py 2</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code> (you write it today), <code>notes/decisions.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 25 min: Build the real file</h2>\n<p>Open <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code>. Leave the big comment at the top. <strong>Below it</strong> write, in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>CREATE OR ALTER VIEW dbo.vw_ReferralsChecked AS</code> then your whole query (the <code>WITH Typed AS ... Numbered AS ...</code> and the final <code>SELECT ... WHERE RowInId = 1</code>). The view must return these columns: <code>ReferralID, ReferredDate, TestType, Priority, SampleReceivedDate, SequencedDate, ReportedDate, RejectReason</code>. A <code>CREATE VIEW</code> cannot be followed directly by other statements, so end it with <code>;</code> and then a line containing just <strong><code>GO</code></strong>.</li>\n<li>Two inserts:\n   - good rows (<code>RejectReason IS NULL</code>) go into <code>dbo.Referrals</code> (columns <code>ReferralID, ReferredDate, TestType, Priority, SampleReceivedDate, SequencedDate, ReportedDate</code>);\n   - bad rows go into <code>dbo.Referrals_Rejected</code> (columns <code>ReferralID, RejectReason</code>).</li>\n<li>A line <code>GO</code>.</li>\n<li>One last <code>SELECT</code> with three numbers named <code>RowsRead</code> (count of <code>dbo.Referrals_Raw</code>), <code>RowsLoaded</code> (count of <code>dbo.Referrals</code>) and <code>RowsRejected</code> (count of <code>dbo.Referrals_Rejected</code>). Use three small subqueries: <code>(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.Referrals_Raw) AS RowsRead</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hint 1 in <code>guide/02</code> shows the shape. The tables <code>dbo.Referrals</code> and <code>dbo.Referrals_Rejected</code> already exist (made in <code>sql/01_create_tables.sql</code>; read the file to see their columns).</p>\n<h2>25 to 45 min: Check</h2>\n<pre><code>python check.py 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are 4 tests. <strong>Read only the first failure message</strong> and fix only that. Common ones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"could not read a statement\": a typo, usually a missing comma between CTEs or a missing <code>;</code>.</li>\n<li>\"view has the wrong number of rows\": you did not trim the id before numbering, or forgot <code>WHERE RowInId = 1</code>.</li>\n<li>wrong reason: your <code>WHEN</code> lines are in the wrong order, or you used <code>&lt;=</code>.</li>\n<li>\"NULLIF\": one of your date conversions has no <code>NULLIF</code> inside <code>TRY_CONVERT</code>. The checker counts them.\nExpected result on the real file, from the brief: <strong>read 8717, loaded 8677, rejected 15</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 7: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>45 to 55 min: Compare with the finished version</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>If you have not already, unzip <code>genomics-demand-capacity.zip</code> (the finished project I sent you earlier) into a <strong>separate</strong> folder.</li>\n<li>Open its <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code> next to yours. Answer, in <code>notes/decisions.md</code> under <strong>## Step 2: cleaning</strong>:\n   - Did I put the <code>WHEN</code>s in a different order? Does that change which reason a row gets?\n   - Which version is easier to read in six months?\n   - What does the finished version do that I did not? What did I do that it does not?</li>\n<li>Add one more entry there: <strong>what is your rule for a duplicate id, and why?</strong> (You thought about it on Days 3 and 6.)</li>\n<li>Say it aloud: <em>\"I load the file as text so a bad value can never stop the load. I convert with TRY_CONVERT after turning empty text into NULL, because SQL Server turns empty text into 1900-01-01. Bad rows are kept in a rejects table with one reason each, so the counts add up: read = loaded + rejected.\"</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>55 to 57 min</h2>\n<p><code>python check.py</code> shows Steps 1 and 2 as PASSED. Tick it on a sticky note. Two steps done.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 7, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 8: open `daily/day-08.md`. Step 3, the weekly table.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-06\">&lt; Day 6</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-08\">Day 8 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-08": {"title": "Day 8: Step 3 (SQL weekly): the idea and the first two parts", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Understand backlog, learn the pattern, and write the four weekly counts.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>guide/03_sql_weekly.md</code>, <code>sql/03_weekly_summary.sql</code> (you write it over Days 8 to 10), new <code>scratch/step3.sql</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 20 min: Think first, on paper</h2>\n<p>Open <code>guide/03_sql_weekly.md</code>. Read down to <strong>The pattern</strong>. Answer on paper:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is a <strong>backlog</strong>? Write it as a sum: <em>samples that have arrived</em> minus <em>samples that have been sequenced</em>, both counted up to the end of the week. Why is it not \"referrals minus sequenced\"? (A referral has no sample yet.)</li>\n<li>If no sample was sequenced in one week, what must that week show: a zero, or no row? Which one breaks a running total?</li>\n<li>The capacity file has 90 weeks. Why does the view show only 78? (Look at <code>data/capacity_weekly.csv</code>'s last 12 rows, and read the brief.)</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>20 to 35 min: Run and read the pattern</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code>python guide/examples/run_library_sql.py</code>. Find the weekly table.</li>\n<li>Open <code>guide/examples/library_sql.sql</code> and read <strong>Pattern 2</strong> (<code>vw_WeeklyLoans</code>). Find the four ideas:</li>\n<li>One small <code>GROUP BY</code> CTE <strong>per measure</strong> (<code>Borrowed</code>, <code>Returned</code>).</li>\n<li>A calendar table is the <strong>left</strong> side. Every week is there. Each measure is <code>LEFT JOIN</code>ed on and wrapped in <code>COALESCE(x, 0)</code>.</li>\n<li>A running total: <code>SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY week ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)</code>. The frame is written out.</li>\n<li>A moving average with <code>ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>, guarded by <code>CASE WHEN COUNT(*) OVER (same frame) = 3</code>.</li>\n<li>Close it.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 8: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>35 to 40 min: Read your brief and one detail</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <code>sql/03_weekly_summary.sql</code>. Read the comment: nine output columns, and three facts.</li>\n<li>Open <code>sql/01_create_tables.sql</code> and find <code>ReferredWeek AS CAST(DATEADD(DAY, (DATEDIFF(DAY, 0, ReferredDate) / 7) * 7, 0) AS DATE)</code>. This gives the <strong>Monday</strong> of the week. Question: why <code>DATEDIFF(DAY, 0, ...)</code> and not <code>DATEPART(WEEKDAY, ...)</code>? (Hint: look up <code>SET DATEFIRST</code>: the setting changes what \"day 1 of the week\" means. <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/datediff-transact-sql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DATEDIFF doc</a>.)</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>40 to 57 min: Parts 1 and 2 in a scratch file</h2>\n<p>Make a file <code>scratch/step3.sql</code>. You will run it with a new switch:</p>\n<pre><code>python run_sql.py --clean scratch/step3.sql\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>--clean</code> fills the table <code>dbo.Referrals</code> with the <strong>known-good clean rows</strong>, so you can build Step 3 without depending on your Step 2. (Without <code>--clean</code> that table is empty.) Your Step 3 query reads <code>dbo.Referrals</code>, exactly as the final file will. The table already has the Monday-of-week columns <code>ReferredWeek</code>, <code>SampleReceivedWeek</code>, <code>SequencedWeek</code>, <code>ReportedWeek</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Part 1: referrals per week.</strong> Type this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH Referred AS (\n    SELECT r.ReferredWeek AS WeekStart, COUNT(*) AS Referrals\n    FROM dbo.Referrals AS r\n    GROUP BY r.ReferredWeek\n)\nSELECT * FROM Referred ORDER BY WeekStart;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run it. <strong>You should see 78 rows</strong>, starting <code>2025-01-06  101</code>, <code>2025-01-13  99</code>, <code>2025-01-20  90</code>, <code>2025-01-27  85</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Part 2: the other three measures.</strong> Write three more CTEs of the same shape, and run each one <strong>alone</strong> first (change the last line to <code>SELECT * FROM &lt;that CTE&gt;</code>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Received</code>: count by <code>SampleReceivedWeek</code>, skipping rows where <code>SampleReceivedDate IS NULL</code>, named <code>SamplesReceived</code>.</li>\n<li><code>Sequenced</code>: count by <code>SequencedWeek</code>, skipping rows with no <code>SequencedDate</code>.</li>\n<li><code>Reported</code>: count by <code>ReportedWeek</code>, skipping rows with no <code>ReportedDate</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why run each alone? When a join later goes wrong you will not know which piece is broken. Small steps find the bug.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 8, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 9: open `daily/day-09.md`. Part 3: join to the capacity calendar, keep 78 weeks.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-07\">&lt; Day 7</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-09\">Day 9 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-09": {"title": "Day 9: Step 3: join the capacity calendar and utilisation (parts 3 and 4)", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Make every week appear, keep exactly 78, and add utilisation without the integer-division trap.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>scratch/step3.sql</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 10 min: Think</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You will start from <code>dbo.CapacityWeekly</code> and <code>LEFT JOIN</code> the four measures <strong>to it</strong>. If you started from a measure, or used <code>INNER JOIN</code>, what would happen to a week with no events?</li>\n<li><code>7 / 2</code> in T-SQL is <strong>3</strong>, not 3.5. Whole number divided by whole number is a whole number. <code>AVG</code> of whole numbers is too. You will see why this matters in Part 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10 to 35 min: Part 3. Join, and keep 78 weeks</h2>\n<p>In <code>scratch/step3.sql</code> add a CTE called <code>Weekly</code> that:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>selects from <code>dbo.CapacityWeekly AS c</code>,</li>\n<li><code>LEFT JOIN</code>s <code>Referred</code>, <code>Received</code>, <code>Sequenced</code> and <code>Reported</code> on the week,</li>\n<li>returns <code>WeekStart</code>, the four measures each wrapped as <code>COALESCE(x.measure, 0)</code>, and <code>c.SequencingCapacity AS Capacity</code>,</li>\n<li>keeps only the first 78 weeks: <code>WHERE c.WeekStart &lt; DATEADD(WEEK, 78, &lt;the first week in the capacity file&gt;)</code>. The first week is <code>(SELECT MIN(WeekStart) FROM dbo.CapacityWeekly)</code>. (Read Hint 2 in <code>guide/03</code> only if you are stuck for 15 minutes.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>End with <code>SELECT * FROM Weekly ORDER BY WeekStart;</code> and run it.\n<strong>You should see 78 rows.</strong> Check:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(*) AS weeks FROM Weekly;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Is any measure NULL? There should be no NULL (that is what <code>COALESCE</code> is for).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 9: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>35 to 55 min: Part 4. Utilisation</h2>\n<p>Utilisation = work done divided by capacity = <code>Sequenced / Capacity</code>, rounded to 3 decimals.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Predict first, on paper.</strong> Suppose one week has <code>Sequenced = 46</code> and <code>Capacity = 100</code>. On SQL Server what is <code>46 / 100</code>? (Answer: <strong>0</strong>. Whole number divided by whole number gives a whole number, so the fraction is thrown away.) And <code>ROUND(46 / 100, 3)</code>? Still 0.</li>\n<li>Write the safe version: <code>ROUND(CAST(w.Sequenced AS DECIMAL(9, 3)) / w.Capacity, 3)</code>. Run it. <strong>Week 1 should show 0.460, week 2 0.930, week 3 1.000.</strong></li>\n<li>Now run the wrong version too, <code>ROUND(w.Sequenced / w.Capacity, 3)</code>, side by side. <strong>You will see the SAME numbers.</strong> That is the trap. DuckDB (the checker's database) divides properly. SQL Server would give 0. So the checker cannot see this mistake, and it only checks that you wrote a <code>CAST</code> or a decimal like <code>1.0</code>. (This is documented T-SQL behaviour. I have not run it on a real SQL Server.)</li>\n<li>On paper, one sentence: why is a mistake that gives the right answer in one database and the wrong answer in another dangerous? How would you protect yourself? (Answer: write decimal arithmetic on purpose, and test on the real server too.)</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>55 to 57 min</h2>\n<p>Save <code>scratch/step3.sql</code>.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 9, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 10: open `daily/day-10.md`. Part 5: backlog and the 4-week average, then move into `sql/03_weekly_summary.sql` and run check 3.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-08\">&lt; Day 8</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-10\">Day 10 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-10": {"title": "Day 10: Step 3: backlog, the 4-week average, check (part 5)", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Finish the weekly view, put it in the real file, and pass <code>python check.py 3</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>scratch/step3.sql</code>, <code>sql/03_weekly_summary.sql</code>, <code>notes/decisions.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 25 min: Part 5. Backlog and the 4-week average</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Backlog at end of week</strong> = (samples received so far) minus (samples sequenced so far). Write one running total for each:\n   <code>sql\n   SUM(w.SamplesReceived) OVER (ORDER BY w.WeekStart ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)</code>\n   and subtract the same for <code>Sequenced</code>. Always write <code>ROWS</code>, not the default <code>RANGE</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Referrals4WkAvg</strong>: the average of <code>Referrals</code> over this week and the 3 before it, 1 decimal, and <strong>NULL for the first 3 weeks</strong>. The frame is <code>ROWS BETWEEN 3 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>. Guard it with <code>CASE WHEN COUNT(*) OVER (the same frame) = 4 THEN ... END</code>. Inside the average use <code>CAST(w.Referrals AS DECIMAL(9, 2))</code> (the same whole-number trap: <code>AVG</code> of whole numbers is a whole number).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Run it with <code>python run_sql.py --clean scratch/step3.sql</code>. <strong>The first four weeks must be:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>WeekStart</th>\n<th>Referrals</th>\n<th>Seq.</th>\n<th>Backlog end</th>\n<th>4-wk avg</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-01-06</td>\n<td>101</td>\n<td>46</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>NULL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-01-13</td>\n<td>99</td>\n<td>93</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>NULL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-01-20</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>NULL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-01-27</td>\n<td>85</td>\n<td>87</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>93.8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(Utilisation for those weeks: 0.46, 0.93, 1.0, 0.87. The last week's backlog is 103.)</p>\n<h2>25 to 40 min: Move it into the real file</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <code>sql/03_weekly_summary.sql</code>. Keep the comment at the top.</li>\n<li>Write <code>CREATE OR ALTER VIEW dbo.vw_WeeklySummary AS</code> and under it copy <strong>your own scratch query</strong> (the CTEs <code>Referred, Received, Sequenced, Reported, Weekly</code> and the final select). It already reads <code>dbo.Referrals</code> and its week columns, so nothing needs changing. Type, do not paste the idea from the guide.</li>\n<li>The final select must return exactly these columns, in this order: <code>WeekStart, Referrals, SamplesReceived, Sequenced, Reported, Capacity, Utilisation, BacklogEnd, Referrals4WkAvg</code>.</li>\n<li>End the view with <code>;</code> then a line <code>GO</code>, and as the very last line of the file: <code>SELECT * FROM dbo.vw_WeeklySummary ORDER BY WeekStart;</code></li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 10: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>40 to 52 min: Check</h2>\n<pre><code>python check.py 3\n</code></pre>\n<p>4 tests. Read the first failure only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"columns and rows\": wrong column names or not 78 rows.</li>\n<li>\"week with no referrals\": you used <code>INNER JOIN</code> or started from a measure. The fix is in Hint 3.</li>\n<li>\"integer division\": add the <code>CAST ... AS DECIMAL</code> (or a <code>1.0 *</code>) in <code>Utilisation</code> and in the average.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>52 to 57 min: Compare and note</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the finished version's <code>sql/03_weekly_summary.sql</code>. What is the same? Four CTEs or one? Is the number 78 written in the SQL? Where would you move it if the history length changed?</li>\n<li><code>notes/decisions.md</code>: add a <strong>Step 3</strong> heading and answer: <strong>why does backlog use samples received and not referrals?</strong></li>\n<li>Say it aloud: <em>\"The weekly view starts from the capacity calendar and left-joins each measure, so a week with no events still shows zero. Backlog is cumulative samples received minus cumulative samples sequenced, using window functions with an explicit frame. I cast to decimal because T-SQL divides whole numbers as whole numbers.\"</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 10, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 11: open `daily/day-11.md`. Step 4, the fairness rule and the eligible view.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-09\">&lt; Day 9</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-11\">Day 11 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-11": {"title": "Day 11: Step 4 (SQL turnaround): the fairness rule and the eligible view", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Understand why only some referrals may be judged, and build the view of those referrals.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>guide/04_sql_turnaround.md</code>, <code>sql/04_turnaround.sql</code>, new <code>scratch/step4.sql</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 20 min: Think first, on paper</h2>\n<p>Open <code>guide/04_sql_turnaround.md</code>, read to <strong>The pattern</strong>. Imagine it is 1 July. A routine referral was made on 25 June. It has no result yet. The target is 42 days.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is that a failure? (No, you cannot tell yet.) If you count it as late, what happens to the latest months on your chart?</li>\n<li>So which referrals may you judge? Write the rule in your own words. The guide's version: <em>a referral can be judged only if it is old enough that its target date has already passed by the end of the data.</em></li>\n<li>An old referral that was <strong>never</strong> reported: within target, or not?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Say your rule out loud. Interviewers like this detail.</p>\n<h2>20 to 30 min: Run and read the pattern</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code>python guide/examples/run_library_sql.py</code>, find <code>PctOnTime</code>.</li>\n<li>In <code>guide/examples/library_sql.sql</code> read <strong>Pattern 3</strong>: a small <code>Rules</code> CTE holds loan days per item type with <code>UNION ALL</code>; a join gives each loan its own limit; a <code>WHERE</code> keeps only loans old enough to judge; <code>SUM(CASE WHEN ...)</code> over <code>COUNT(*)</code> gives the share. Close it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>30 to 40 min: Read your brief and numbers</h2>\n<p>Open <code>sql/04_turnaround.sql</code>. Three parts. Write on paper:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Targets (assumptions, <strong>not NHS standards</strong>): urgent <strong>14</strong> days, routine <strong>42</strong> days.</li>\n<li><code>DataEnd</code> = the first referral date + 78 weeks. (For this data: first date 2025-01-06, so DataEnd is 2026-07-06.)</li>\n<li>Eligible when <code>ReferredDate &lt;= DataEnd - TargetDays</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 11: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>40 to 57 min: Part 1. The view</h2>\n<p>Make <code>scratch/step4.sql</code> and run it with <code>python run_sql.py --clean scratch/step4.sql</code> (<code>--clean</code> fills <code>dbo.Referrals</code> with the known-good clean rows, as on Day 8). Type this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH Params  AS (SELECT DATEADD(WEEK, 78, MIN(r.ReferredDate)) AS DataEnd FROM dbo.Referrals AS r),\nTargets AS (SELECT 'urgent' AS Priority, 14 AS TargetDays UNION ALL SELECT 'routine', 42),\nEligible AS (\n    SELECT r.Priority, t.TargetDays,\n           DATEFROMPARTS(YEAR(r.ReferredDate), MONTH(r.ReferredDate), 1) AS ReferralMonth,\n           DATEDIFF(DAY, r.ReferredDate, r.ReportedDate) AS DaysToReport\n    FROM dbo.Referrals AS r\n    INNER JOIN Targets AS t ON t.Priority = r.Priority\n    CROSS JOIN Params AS p\n    WHERE r.ReferredDate &lt;= DATEADD(DAY, -t.TargetDays, p.DataEnd)\n)\nSELECT Priority, COUNT(*) AS n FROM Eligible GROUP BY Priority ORDER BY Priority DESC;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Read every line and say out loud what it does (this is Hint 1 in <code>guide/04</code>, so try to explain it in your own words).</p>\n<p><strong>You should see:</strong> <code>urgent 1216</code> and <code>routine 6819</code>. That is 8,035 eligible referrals out of the 8,677 clean ones.</p>\n<p>Now do <strong>one experiment</strong> so the rule sticks: change <code>42</code> to <code>1</code>, run it again. <strong>Routine goes up to 7420.</strong> Why? (A shorter target means more referrals are old enough to judge.) Change it back to <code>42</code>.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 11, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 12: open `daily/day-12.md`. Parts 2 and 3: PctWithinTarget, then median and p90.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-10\">&lt; Day 10</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-12\">Day 12 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-12": {"title": "Day 12: Step 4: percentage within target, median and p90 (parts 2 and 3)", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Compute the share within target, and the median and 90th percentile, per month and priority.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>scratch/step4.sql</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 10 min: Three traps (read, do not skip)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code>100 * SUM(...) / COUNT(*)</code> is whole-number maths in T-SQL. Start with <strong><code>100.0 *</code></strong>.</li>\n<li>In T-SQL, <code>PERCENTILE_CONT</code> needs <code>OVER</code>. It returns one value <strong>per row</strong>. Use <code>SELECT DISTINCT</code> to get one row per group. (In other databases it is a normal aggregate; do not carry the habit.) <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/percentile-cont-transact-sql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PERCENTILE_CONT doc</a>.</li>\n<li><code>NULL &lt;= 14</code> is not true. An unreported referral has <code>DaysToReport = NULL</code>, so <code>CASE WHEN DaysToReport &lt;= TargetDays THEN 1 ELSE 0 END</code> gives 0 for it. That is the behaviour you want. Be able to say <strong>why it works</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>10 to 30 min: Part 2. Percentage within target</h2>\n<p>In yesterday's <code>scratch/step4.sql</code> replace the last line (<code>SELECT Priority, COUNT(*) ...</code>) with the query below, and run it with <code>python run_sql.py --clean scratch/step4.sql</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT e.ReferralMonth, e.Priority, COUNT(*) AS Referrals,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN e.DaysToReport &lt;= e.TargetDays THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*), 1) AS PctWithinTarget\nFROM Eligible AS e\nGROUP BY e.ReferralMonth, e.Priority\nORDER BY e.Priority DESC, e.ReferralMonth;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Type it yourself and read every line. (<code>DESC</code> puts <code>urgent</code> before <code>routine</code>: look at the letters.)\n<strong>The first rows of the finished answer are:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>month</th>\n<th>priority</th>\n<th>referrals</th>\n<th>% within target</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-01-01</td>\n<td>urgent</td>\n<td>56</td>\n<td>100.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-02-01</td>\n<td>urgent</td>\n<td>58</td>\n<td>100.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2025-03-01</td>\n<td>urgent</td>\n<td>73</td>\n<td>100.0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(35 rows in all: 18 months for urgent and 17 for routine. Routine stops a month earlier because its target is longer, so the latest routine month is not old enough to judge. Compare your first rows with the table above.)</p>\n<h2>30 to 50 min: Part 3. Median and p90</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT DISTINCT\n    e.ReferralMonth, e.Priority,\n    PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY e.DaysToReport) OVER (PARTITION BY e.Priority, e.ReferralMonth) AS MedianDays,\n    PERCENTILE_CONT(0.9) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY e.DaysToReport) OVER (PARTITION BY e.Priority, e.ReferralMonth) AS P90Days\nFROM Eligible AS e\nORDER BY e.Priority DESC, e.ReferralMonth;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Type it. You should get <strong>35 rows</strong>. Now <strong>break it on purpose</strong>: remove the word <code>DISTINCT</code> and run it. <strong>You get 8,035 rows</strong> (one per eligible referral). Why? Put <code>DISTINCT</code> back.\nQuestions to answer on paper:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rows not yet reported have no <code>DaysToReport</code>. What does <code>PERCENTILE_CONT</code> do with them? (It ignores NULLs.)</li>\n<li>Why are the median and the 90th percentile useful next to a percentage? (A percentage tells you <em>how many</em> missed; a percentile tells you <em>how late</em> the slow ones are.)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 12: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>50 to 57 min: Put it into the real file</h2>\n<p>Open <code>sql/04_turnaround.sql</code>. Keep the brief comment. Write: the view <code>dbo.vw_EligibleForTarget</code> (the <code>Params</code>, <code>Targets</code> and eligible <code>SELECT</code> from your scratch, without the final count line; the view's columns are <code>Priority, TargetDays, ReferralMonth, DaysToReport</code>), <code>;</code>, <code>GO</code>, then the two selects reading <code>FROM dbo.vw_EligibleForTarget AS e</code>. Do <strong>not</strong> run the check yet. That is tomorrow.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 12, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 13: open `daily/day-13.md`. Run `python check.py 4` and fix.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-11\">&lt; Day 11</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-13\">Day 13 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-13": {"title": "Day 13: Step 4: check and Stage A review", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Pass <code>python check.py 4</code>, then confirm the whole SQL half is done.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> <code>sql/04_turnaround.sql</code>, <code>notes/decisions.md</code>.</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 30 min: Check 4</h2>\n<pre><code>python check.py 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Five tests. Read the <strong>first</strong> failure only. Likely ones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"view holds only eligible referrals\": your <code>WHERE</code> is wrong. Eligible when <code>ReferredDate &lt;= DATEADD(DAY, -TargetDays, DataEnd)</code>. Is <code>DataEnd</code> built from <code>MIN(ReferredDate)</code> plus 78 weeks?</li>\n<li>\"no integer division in the percentage\": you forgot <code>100.0 *</code>.</li>\n<li>\"rules on a small table\": test these questions: is an unreported old referral counted as <em>not</em> within target? Are you counting <code>NULL</code> days as within target by mistake?</li>\n<li>\"median and p90\": did you leave out <code>DISTINCT</code> or use the wrong <code>PARTITION BY</code>?</li>\n<li>If the checker says it <strong>cannot read a statement</strong>: look for a typo first. If the T-SQL is valid, tell me. I can add support.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>30 to 45 min: Compare and decide</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the finished version's <code>sql/04_turnaround.sql</code>. How does it hold the targets? What would you change to move the targets into a table you can edit? Are there other ways to apply the eligibility rule?</li>\n<li>In <code>notes/decisions.md</code> under <strong>## Step 4: turnaround</strong> answer: <strong>what would you tell a manager who asks why the latest month has no figure?</strong></li>\n<li>Say it aloud: <em>\"The turnaround measure only counts referrals that are old enough to have met their target by the end of the data. Without that, recent referrals look late only because they have not finished, and the last months of the chart would be wrong. Unreported old referrals count as missing the target.\"</em></li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 13: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>45 to 55 min: Stage A review</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code>python check.py</code> should show <strong>Steps 1, 2, 3 and 4 PASSED</strong> and 5 to 10 \"not yet\". If one is not, fix it <strong>now</strong>.</li>\n<li>Read the last section of <code>guide/04</code> (<strong>Stage A is done</strong>).</li>\n<li>On paper, write the <strong>five things you learned in the SQL half that you did not know a fortnight ago</strong>. (Examples: why <code>NULLIF</code> goes inside <code>TRY_CONVERT</code>; why backlog is a running difference; why <code>PERCENTILE_CONT</code> needs <code>OVER</code>.)</li>\n<li>Optional: upload the SQL half to GitHub now (the web steps are at the end of <code>guide/11_readme_and_interview.md</code>). Upload <code>sql/</code>, <code>notes/</code>, <code>guide/</code>, <code>README.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>55 to 57 min</h2>\n<p>Tomorrow is a <strong>blank-page session</strong>. You will write from memory. Do not read your own SQL tonight.</p>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 13, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 14: open `daily/day-14.md`. Blank-page session.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-12\">&lt; Day 12</a>  <a href=\"#/genomics/day-14\">Day 14 &gt;</a></p>"}, "day-14": {"title": "Day 14: Blank-page session: rebuild from memory", "kind": "day", "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Short on time?</strong> Part 1 is everything above the PAUSE POINT (roughly 35 to 50 minutes, depending on the day). Part 2 is the rest. If your project time runs out earlier, stop at the end of the section you are in and log its title. Brick wall? See <code>STAY-ON-TRACK.md</code> section 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Goal today:</strong> Find out what you really know, without looking, then fix the gaps.</p>\n<p><strong>Files you will touch:</strong> A new empty file <code>scratch/blank_1.sql</code>, and <code>gotchas-cheatsheet.md</code> (your cheat sheet).</p>\n<h2>0 to 5 min: Start (the same every day)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the project folder <code>genomics-demand-capacity-guided</code> in File Explorer.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>address bar</strong> at the top of the window, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter. A black window opens <strong>inside that folder</strong>. (Mac: open Terminal, type <code>cd</code> and drag the folder onto the window, press Enter.)</li>\n<li>Type <code>python check.py</code> and press Enter. (If you see \"python is not recognized\", type <code>py check.py</code> instead, and use <code>py</code> wherever this page says <code>python</code>.)</li>\n<li>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> in your text editor (VS Code if you have it, otherwise Notepad). Read your <strong>last line</strong>. It says where you stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5 to 10 min: The rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Do not open</strong> <code>sql/02_clean_load.sql</code>, <code>sql/03_...</code>, <code>sql/04_...</code>, or the guide. Close them.</li>\n<li>You may look at <code>guide/examples/library_sql.sql</code> (a different subject) if you are truly stuck after 10 minutes.</li>\n<li>Wrong is fine. The mistakes tell us what to teach next.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10 to 35 min: Task A (from a blank file)</h2>\n<p>Create <code>scratch/blank_1.sql</code>. Write, from memory, a query on <code>dbo.Referrals_Raw</code> that returns <strong>one row per ReferralID</strong> (so the 25 repeats disappear) with these columns: <code>ReferralID</code> (trimmed), <code>ReferredDate</code> (a real date, empty becomes NULL), <code>Priority</code> (trimmed, lower case) and <code>RowInId</code>. Keep only <code>RowInId = 1</code>. Then end it with a <code>COUNT(*)</code>.</p>\n<p>Run it: <code>python run_sql.py scratch/blank_1.sql</code>. <strong>You should get 8692.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2><strong>PAUSE POINT.</strong> This is the nearest tidy stopping place. If you run out of project time (35 minutes) before here, stop at the end of the section you are in instead. Write one line in <code>notes/progress.md</code>: <code>Day 14: done up to &lt;section title&gt;. Resume there.</code> Tomorrow, start at the section below. (Do the 'Start' steps first, as always, but not again the parts you already did.)</h2>\n<h2>35 to 50 min: Task B (small)</h2>\n<p>Write a second query, from memory, on <code>dbo.CapacityWeekly</code>: for each week, the <strong>running total of capacity</strong> and the <strong>average capacity over this week and the 3 before it, NULL for the first 3 weeks</strong>. (You wrote the same idea in Step 3.) Check that the row count is 90 and the first four averages are NULL.</p>\n<h2>50 to 57 min: Learn from it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Now (only now) open your Step 2 and Step 3 files and compare. On paper write <strong>the five mistakes you made most</strong> across Days 5 to 12, in your own words. Add them to <code>gotchas-cheatsheet.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Write one sentence each for: what is <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> for? What does <code>ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING</code> mean? What does <code>NULLIF(x, '')</code> do?</li>\n<li>If Task A or B took you more than 25 minutes, <strong>repeat the one you got wrong tomorrow</strong> before moving on. Do not rush into pandas.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Last 3 min: Log it (do not skip)</h2>\n<p>Open <code>notes/progress.md</code> and add ONE line at the bottom, in this form:</p>\n<pre><code>Session 14, &lt;date&gt;: &lt;what I did&gt;. Stuck on: &lt;nothing, or what&gt;. Tomorrow start with: Day 15: open `daily/day-15.md` (course lesson 3.1). Not written yet if this line is still here: tell your tutor.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Save the file. <strong>Stop at 3 pm</strong>, even in the middle of something. A half-finished step is fine if the next action is written down.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"#/genomics/day-13\">&lt; Day 13</a>  Day 15 &gt;</p>"}}};
